# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Expo / Metro
web-build/
.expo/

# OS and editor
.DS_Store
*.log

# Secrets. These were deliberately tracked once, so a clone would be runnable
# as-is, on the reasoning that the repository was private. That trade stopped
# being worth it: the file now carries an Anthropic API key, which is billable
# on sight, alongside the Supabase service-role key, which bypasses row-level
# security entirely. Neither is a thing to hand to everyone who clones.
#
# Ignoring it does not unpublish it. `packages/server/.env` is in the history
# and the service-role key in it should be treated as compromised and rotated —
# along with anything else that was ever committed here.
#
# `packages/server/.env.example` stays tracked, so a clone still knows what it
# needs. So does `packages/app/.env`, which holds one `EXPO_PUBLIC_` variable —
# that prefix means Expo compiles it into the client bundle, so it is published
# to every visitor by definition and there is nothing in it to protect.
.env
packages/server/.env
!.env.example
!packages/*/.env.example
!packages/app/.env

# Fetched product imagery. Every file here is re-downloadable from the source
# on demand — the media route regenerates a derivative on first request — so
# carrying tens of megabytes of third-party product photos in git history buys
# nothing a clone cannot get for itself. The handful of generated placeholders
# committed earlier stay tracked; only the fetched originals are ignored.
**/.data/media/f_*/

# Cookie jars the marketplace fetchers prime before a crawl. These hold live
# session tokens for amazon.com and ebay.com, they are re-minted on demand, and
# they are per-machine — so unlike the generated media above they must never be
# committed.
**/.data/cookies/
